          "source_quote": "τοὺς ἀληθείᾳ διαφέροντας μὴ ἐπὶ πλεῖον εἰσφέρειν λόγους",
          "english_quote": "and would thus not have introduced the matters important to truth more extensively.",
          "issue": "“Matters” obscures λόγους, the arguments or discourses being presented. Earlier, περὶ τῶν τῇ ἀληθείᾳ διαφερόντων denotes the matters about which declarations were made; here the source explicitly identifies the truth-relevant discourses themselves. The draft collapses that distinction.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use wording such as “and would thus not have presented the arguments important to truth more extensively.” Preserve the distinction between subjects of discussion and the arguments addressing them. The replacement requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "17.7",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "διὸ κρίνας εἶναι τὸ πᾶν αὐτὸν καὶ τὴν αὐτοῦ εἰκόνα τὸν ἄνθρωπον",
          "english_quote": "Therefore, having determined that the whole should be himself and the human being his image",
          "issue": "The subject of κρίνας and the referent of “himself” are not adequately handled. The following grammatical subject is ὁ αὐτὸν σέβειν θέλων, the person wishing to revere God. On that attachment, the sense is that this person judges God to be the whole, not that the person determines the whole should be himself. Continuing God as the subject from the preceding sentence is another possible contextual analysis, but the English leaves a dangling divine determination attached grammatically to the worshipper. The note discusses the relation between God and the whole, not this consequential subject ambiguity.",
          "suggested_revision": "One defensible construction is “Therefore, judging him to be the whole and the human being his image … whoever wishes to revere him honors his visible image.” Explain that this attaches κρίνας to the worshipper; preserve the alternative analysis continuing God as the subject of a determination. If choosing that alternative in the main text, identify its subject explicitly rather than leaving “himself” attached to the worshipper. Submit the revised sentence and note for fresh review.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "17.8",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ὃ ἂν καὶ αὐτοῦ πληροῦν τὸ κενόν",
          "english_quote": "which would itself also fill the void?",
          "issue": "The draft treats αὐτοῦ as though it were the nominative emphatic αὐτό, “itself.” The supplied αὐτοῦ can instead be locative, “there,” or genitive, “of it.” Neither its form nor this attachment question is addressed in the notes. This is a translation problem in the verified supplied reading, not evidence of an OCR error.",
          "suggested_revision": "A defensible locative rendering is “which would also fill the void there?” Explain the locative interpretation of αὐτοῦ and preserve the possible genitive alternative, “its void,” with the relevant referent left qualified where necessary. Do not silently substitute αὐτό for the supplied αὐτοῦ. The revised wording requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "17.9",
      "verdict": "accept",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": true,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": true
      },
      "findings": []
    },
    {
      "reference": "17.10",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": true,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "εἰς τὸν αὐτοῦ κόλπον φέρονται, ὡς ἐν χειμῶνι ὥρας οἱ ἀτμοὶ τῶν ὀρῶν ὑπὸ τῶν τοῦ ἡλίου ἀκτίνων ἑλκόμενοι φέρονται πρὸς αὐτὸν ἀθάνατοι.",
          "english_quote": "they are carried immortal into his embrace, just as in winter the vapors of the mountains, drawn by the sun’s rays, are carried toward the sun.",
          "issue": "Moving “immortal” before the simile explicitly attaches ἀθάνατοι to the souls. That is contextually defensible, but the adjective can grammatically agree with either αἱ ψυχαί or οἱ ἀτμοί, and its printed position follows the statement about the vapors. The notes do not disclose this material attachment alternative. The uncertainty does not make the main rendering unsupported or require source verification.",
          "suggested_revision": "Add a source-quoted note explaining that “immortal” is attached to the souls because the passage concerns their life after separation from the body. Preserve the alternative local attachment: “the vapors … are carried toward the sun, immortal.” The main wording may remain proposed, but the updated passage and disclosure require fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
